In the case of wave propagation in a homogeneous half-space, surface waves, particularly the Rayleigh waves [ 6 ,  7 ], emerge around the free surface. The Rayleigh waves form due to the reflection of shear wave at the surface and propagates along the surface through elliptical particle motions. Similar to body waves, the Rayleigh waves in a homogeneous half-space is non-dispersive, which means its velocity does not vary with frequency. Due to the challenges associated with transmissions, reflections, and conversions at the surface, only approximate relationships are available to estimate the Rayleigh wave speed  c R \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
